Directional pattern worth flagging, verified precisely (not eyeballed) via a
separate breakdown pass: of the 9 misses, 6 found claim_b (the later/
corrected side) but not claim_a (the original, now-superseded side); 1 found
the reverse (claim_a but not claim_b); 2 found neither. The 6-vs-1 skew is a
real, if modest at this n, directional signal that the superseding claim is
easier to find than the superseded one -- makes sense (newer/current facts
get referenced and consolidated more; the old claim stops being reinforced
once corrected) and is directly actionable: a future gather-loop follow-up
round specifically needs to search for EARLIER assertions on a topic, not
just more recent content about it, since "more of the same" biases toward
finding the side that's already easy to find. This asymmetry claim itself
has an even smaller n (9 misses) than the headline number -- treat it as a
hypothesis worth testing at scale, not a confirmed law.
